    <string name="explore_title">Explore Wolaita</string>
    <string name="explore_intro_title">Introduction and History</string>
    <string name="explore_intro_text"><![CDATA[The Wolayta people have a rich history, with over 50 kings (Kawo) from three dynasties. Before adopting the Ethiopian Birr, they had their own currency called Marchuwa. The Wolayta are known for their hospitality, patriotism, strong work ethic, and vibrant cultural expressions, including music, food, dance, and fine craftsmanship.]]></string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_title">Wolayta Travel Guide</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_air_title">By Air</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_air_text">The most convenient way to reach Wolayta is by flying from Addis Ababa to Arba Minch via Ethiopian Airlines, followed by a 105 km road trip to Sodo.</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_road_title">By Road</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_road_text">Multiple bus and taxi services run from Addis Ababa to Sodo daily. The most common routes are via Butajira–Alaba (330 km), Butajira–Hossana (339 km), or Mojo–Ziway–Shashemene (380 km).</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_population_title">Population</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_population_text">The population of Wolayta is over 5 million, with a density of 385 people per square kilometer.</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_weather_title">Climate</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_weather_text">Wolayta enjoys a mild and pleasant climate. The average annual temperature is 15.1°C, with rainfall ranging from 1,200 to 1,300 mm.</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_language_title">Language</string>
    <string name="explore_tour_guide_language_text">The Wolayta language, “Wolayttatto,” is spoken by over 5 million people and belongs to the Omo-Tigrean language family.</string>
    <string name="explore_attractions_title">Tourist Attractions in Wolayta</string>
    <string name="explore_attractions_text">From the Mochena Borago Cave, inhabited 58,000–70,000 years ago, to the stunning Ajora Twin Waterfalls and the Abela Chokare hot springs, Wolayta offers a range of unforgettable attractions.</string>
    <string name="explore_gallery_title">Wolayta Attractions Gallery</string>
    <string name="explore_gallery_text">Browse images of the natural and cultural wonders of Wolayta.</string>
    <string name="explore_gallery_play">Play Slideshow</string>
    <string name="explore_gallery_pause">Pause Slideshow</string>
